', 'https://content.rawkode.academy/courses/graphql-federation-wundergraph-cosmo/graphql-federation-wundergraph-cosmo.png', 'GraphQL Federation with WunderGraph Cosmo Course Cover', 1280, 720, 1753315200, 'advanced', '["Learn federation concepts and schema design","Build and compose subgraphs with Cosmo","Optimize, observe, and deploy to production"]', '["rawkode"]', '[]', '[{"title":"WunderGraph Cosmo Documentation","description":"Official WunderGraph Cosmo documentation","type":"url","url":"https://cosmo-docs.wundergraph.com/","category":"documentation"},{"title":"GraphQL Federation Specification","description":"Apollo Federation specification and concepts","type":"url","url":"https://www.apollographql.com/docs/federation/","category":"documentation"},{"title":"WunderGraph GitHub Repository","description":"Source code and examples for WunderGraph","type":"url","url":"https://github.com/wundergraph/wundergraph","category":"code"},{"title":"Cosmo Examples","description":"Example projects using WunderGraph Cosmo","type":"url","url":"https://github.com/wundergraph/cosmo/tree/main/examples","category":"code"}]', '{"audienceId":"6f77a8d7-77d5-433c-aacb-21392e3f2c9f","sponsor":"WunderGraph","sponsorAudienceId":"eae7901e-18dd-4c19-a7d5-b038688595f5","allowSponsorContact":true}', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"about-this-course"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"About this Course"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"GraphQL Federation has become the de facto standard for building scalable, distributed GraphQL architectures. In this advanced course, we''ll explore how to leverage WunderGraph Cosmo to build, deploy, and manage federated GraphQL services at scale."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You''ll learn how to:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Design and implement federated GraphQL schemas"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Build and compose multiple GraphQL subgraphs"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Handle authentication and authorization across federated services"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Optimize performance with caching and query planning"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Monitor and debug distributed GraphQL systems"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deploy and scale federated architectures in production"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Whether you''re migrating from a monolithic GraphQL API or building a new distributed system from scratch, this course will provide you with the knowledge and practical skills needed to succeed with GraphQL Federation using WunderGraph Cosmo."}]}]}', 1766435435000);
', 'https://content.rawkode.academy/courses/rust-for-the-rusty/rust-for-the-rusty.jpg', 'Rust for the Rusty Course Cover', 1376, 768, 1766016000, 'beginner', '["Borrow checker, who dis?","Once in a ''lifetime","let the_games_begin = true;"]', '["icepuma"]', '["rust"]', NULL, NULL, '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"about-this-course"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"About this Course"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I''m writing Rust with the help of all sorts of coding agents, I understand most of the written code but wanted to relearn everything from scratch. So I try to teach myself and others the nooks and crannies of Rust. The modules will be 5-10 minutes so you can easily do one module per day / week."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"a-few-more-words"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A few more words"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I''ll do most of the examples on macOS, so some calls might differ on your system."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I''d appreciate telling me what I can improve about this course, feel free to"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"either open a PR "},{"type":"text","text":"here","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://github.com/rawkode-academy/rawkode-academy/issues/new","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"or open a discussion "},{"type":"text","text":"here","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://github.com/orgs/rawkode-academy/discussions/new?category=q-a","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}', 1769562872000);
From zero to a fully working setup with identity-based access, role-based controls, just-in-time
privilege escalation, and full audit logging.
', 'https://content.rawkode.academy/courses/teleport-for-kubernetes/teleport-for-kubernetes.png', 'Teleport for Kubernetes Course Cover', 1376, 768, 1774224000, 'intermediate', '["Deploy Teleport on Kubernetes with Helm","Configure identity-based access with GitHub SSO","Implement fine-grained RBAC with defense in depth","Set up just-in-time access requests for incidents","Use audit logging and session recording"]', '["rawkode"]', '["teleport","kubernetes"]', '[{"title":"Teleport Documentation","description":"Official Teleport documentation","type":"url","url":"https://goteleport.com/docs/","category":"documentation"},{"title":"Teleport GitHub Repository","description":"Source code and community edition","type":"url","url":"https://github.com/gravitational/teleport","category":"code"}]', '{"audienceId":"teleport-for-kubernetes","sponsor":"Teleport","sponsorAudienceId":"teleport","allowSponsorContact":true}', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"about-this-course"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"About this Course"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kubernetes access is broken by default. Long-lived kubeconfig credentials, shared identities, no revocation, and audit logs that can''t tell you who did what. This course fixes that."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Over six hands-on videos, you''ll deploy Teleport on Kubernetes from scratch using Helm, wire up GitHub SSO for identity-based authentication, build a two-layer RBAC model with defense in depth, implement just-in-time access requests for incident response, and explore Teleport''s audit logging and session recording capabilities."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything is built on the Teleport Community Edition, which is free and open source. The video walkthroughs demonstrate the setup on a local Kind cluster — no cloud account required."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"By the end of this course, you''ll have the foundations for securing your Kubernetes access with real identities, least privilege, time-bounded elevation, and a complete audit trail."}]}]}', 1790105224000);
', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Getting Started', 1, '{"id":"hgg3yhgkk4jn9l7ryjw9ebs8","thumbnailUrl":"https://content.rawkode.academy/videos/hgg3yhgkk4jn9l7ryjw9ebs8/thumbnail.jpg"}', NULL, NULL, 1751414400, 0, '["rawkode"]', '[{"title":"Zitadel Documentation","description":"Official Zitadel documentation","type":"url","url":"https://zitadel.com/docs","category":"documentation"},{"title":"Zitadel GitHub Repository","description":"Source code and examples","type":"url","url":"https://github.com/zitadel/zitadel","category":"code"},{"title":"Authentication Basics","description":"Understanding OAuth2 and OpenID Connect","type":"url","url":"https://oauth.net/2/","category":"documentation"},{"title":"OAuth PKCE Interactive Demo","description":"Try the OAuth PKCE flow right in your browser - Enter your Zitadel instance URL and client ID to test authentication","type":"embed","embedConfig":{"container":"webcontainer","src":"oauth-pkce-app","height":"800px","width":"100%","import":{"localDir":"complete-guide-zitadel/oauth-pkce-app"},"startCommand":"bun run dev"},"category":"code"}]', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"welcome-to-the-complete-guide-to-zitadel"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Welcome to the Complete Guide to Zitadel"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sick of building authentication from scratch or getting nickeled and dimed by expensive identity providers? This practical Zitadel course shows you how to implement enterprise-grade authentication without the enterprise price tag. Learn to set up and integrate Zitadel - the open source identity and access management platform written in Go - with your applications. Covers OAuth, SAML, user management, multi-tenancy, custom branding, user flows, and production deployment. No theory dumps, just hands-on implementation from David Flanagan at Rawkode Academy, who actually uses Zitadel in production. Perfect for developers and sysadmins who want authentication that works without breaking budgets or sanity."}]}]}', 1790105224000);
', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Getting Started', 1, NULL, NULL, NULL, 1769558400, 0, '["icepuma"]', '[{"title":"The Rust Programming Language (The Book)","description":"The official Rust book for fundamentals and deeper context","type":"url","url":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/","category":"documentation"}]', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"welcome"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Welcome"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Let the journey begin. We''re walking through the depths of the compiler, touch the build ecosystem, get up to speed with the features of the language to end up writing our own small WASM runtime. I think the best way to start things is to talk a bit about the history of Rust."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"the-history-of-rust"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The history of Rust"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Like many languages, Rust started out as a hobby project. Here''s a small timeline of notable milestones:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2006"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Graydon Hoare starts Rust as his pet project while working at Mozilla"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rust was initially written in OCaml"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2009"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mozilla offially sponsors Rust for a potential safe browser engine"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The OCaml based compiler is switched to an LLVM based backend"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2012"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rust 0.1 is released for Windows, Linux and macOS"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2015"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rust 1.0 drops"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2016"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The first version of Servo is released"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Servo was developed in parallel with Rust. Both ecosystems participated from each other"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2021"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Rust foundation is founded by AWS, Google, Huawei, Microsoft and Mozilla"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here''s a small list of resources you can read more in-depth about the history of Rust:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Steve Klabnik - History of Rust","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://steveklabnik.github.io/history-of-rust/","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Graydon Hoare - 10 Years of Stable Rust: An Infrastructure Story","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://rustfoundation.org/media/10-years-of-stable-rust-an-infrastructure-story/","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Clive Thompson - How Rust went from a side project to the world''s most-loved programming language","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.technologyreview.com/2023/02/14/1067869/rust-worlds-fastest-growing-programming-language/","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"releases.rs - Rust Changelogs","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://releases.rs","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"how-to-get-started-with-the-development-of-rust"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to get started with the development of Rust"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now let''s get our hands dirty and install Rust on our machine. I usually setup a new Rust installation with "},{"type":"text","text":"Rustup","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://rustup.rs","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}]},{"type":"text","text":". As I''m on macOS I use "},{"type":"text","text":"homebrew","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://rust-lang.github.io/rustup/installation/other.html#homebrew","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}]},{"type":"text","text":", but there''s a lot more "},{"type":"text","text":"installation methods","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://rust-lang.github.io/rustup/installation/other.html#other-installation-methods","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}]},{"type":"text","text":" you can choose from."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rust has different release channels ("},{"type":"text","text":"stable","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is the default one, there''s also "},{"type":"text","text":"beta","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"nightly","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":"), so running "},{"type":"text","text":"rustup default stable","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" for the first time installs the "},{"type":"text","text":"stable","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" rust toolchain with tools like "},{"type":"text","text":"cargo","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"rustc","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and more. After setting up the toolchain, we can run "},{"type":"text","text":"rustc --version","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" to reveal the version of the Rust compiler."}]},{"type":"blockquote","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"When you see the message \"error: linker "},{"type":"text","text":"cc","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" not found\", you have to install the "},{"type":"text","text":"C","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" linker e.g. by running "},{"type":"text","text":"apt install cmake","marks":[{"type":"code"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"To see if the Rust compiler really works, we can write a small \"hello rust\" program, compile and run it:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"shell","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"echo ''fn main() { println!(\"🦀\"); }'' > hello_rust.rs\nrustc hello_rust.rs\n./hello_rust\n🦀"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"As you notice, we have to surround the "},{"type":"text","text":"println!()","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" with a "},{"type":"text","text":"main","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" function to run properly. What happens when we run "},{"type":"text","text":"rustc","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" with the code without the function:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"shell","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"rustc hello_rust.rs\nerror: macro expansion ignores `{` and any tokens following\n   --> /Users/icepuma/.rustup/toolchains/stable-aarch64-apple-darwin/lib/rustlib/src/rust/library/std/src/macros.rs:142:23\n    |\n142 |     ($($arg:tt)*) => {{\n    |                       ^\n    |\n   ::: hello_rust.rs:1:1\n    |\n  1 | println!(\"🦀\");\n    | -------------- caused by the macro expansion here\n    |\n    = note: the usage of `println!` is likely invalid in item context\n\nerror[E0601]: `main` function not found in crate `hello_rust`\n --> hello_rust.rs:1:15\n  |\n1 | println!(\"🦀\");\n  |                ^ consider adding a `main` function to `hello_rust.rs`\n\nerror: aborting due to 2 previous errors\n\nFor more information about this error, try `rustc --explain E0601`."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We ignore the first error for now (we''ll cover macros later). The one cool thing about the Rust compiler is, that we get really informative error messages. For example, if we run "},{"type":"text","text":"rustc --explain E0601","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", we get:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"shell","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"No main function was found in a binary crate.\n\nTo fix this error, add a main function:\n\nfn main() {\n    // Your program will start here.\n    println!(\"Hello world!\");\n}\n\nIf you don''t know the basics of Rust, you can look at the Rust Book to get started."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rust programs are organized in \"crates\", where the "},{"type":"text","text":"main","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" function is the entry point for a binary crate (e.g. our "},{"type":"text","text":"hello_rust.rs","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":"). Rust also has \"library\" crates, which should not contain a \"main\" function (it is possible, but it''s dead code unless you explicitely call it). Libraries are typically used to distribute code, that might be used as a dependency in another crate."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now we''re able to compile and run Rust code. Let''s see what''s next :)"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"whats-next"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What''s next"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Next module will be all about "},{"type":"text","text":"cargo","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and the Rust toolchain."}]}]}', 1769562872000);
', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Getting Started', 1, '{"id":"qhc6jajwhi2ul563ncovdrg9","thumbnailUrl":"https://content.rawkode.academy/videos/qhc6jajwhi2ul563ncovdrg9/thumbnail.webp"}', NULL, NULL, 1774224000, 0, '["rawkode"]', '[{"title":"Teleport Documentation","description":"Official Teleport documentation","type":"url","url":"https://goteleport.com/docs/","category":"documentation"},{"title":"Teleport GitHub Repository","description":"Source code and community edition","type":"url","url":"https://github.com/gravitational/teleport","category":"code"}]', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"welcome-to-teleport-for-kubernetes"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Welcome to Teleport for Kubernetes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Over the next six videos, we go from zero to a fully working setup — identity-based access, role-based controls, just-in-time privilege escalation, and full audit logging. Everything you need to lock down Kubernetes access the right way."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"the-problem"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Problem"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If you''ve been working with Kubernetes, you''re familiar with kubeconfig files. The standard way to authenticate with any Kubernetes cluster — you get the file, drop it into "},{"type":"text","text":"~/.kube/config","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":", and off you go. But have you ever looked inside a kubeconfig file?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typically, your kubeconfig contains a long-lived secret. These certificates live for months or years with no ability to revoke them unless you rotate the entire certificate authority of your cluster. The subject is always generic — "},{"type":"text","text":"kubernetes-admin","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — because Kubernetes has no real concept of users."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This creates audit gaps: when everyone shares the same identity, it''s hard to tell who did what. And in our AI landscape, your AI agents potentially have access to all of your clusters with the same permissions you have."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"the-solution-teleport"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Solution: Teleport"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Teleport is an infrastructure access platform that sits in front of your Kubernetes clusters, SSH servers, databases, and web applications. It provides a single unified layer for authentication and authorization with complete audit capabilities."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Instead of handing out long-lived credentials, Teleport acts as your certificate authority. When you authenticate through your company''s SSO — Okta, Google Workspace, or GitHub — Teleport issues you a short-lived x509 certificate containing your real identity, roles, and an expiration time. By default, certificates are valid for around 12 hours."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"how-teleport-works"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How Teleport Works"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under the hood, there are three core services:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Auth Service","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The brain of Teleport. It runs a certificate authority, stores cluster state, users, roles, connectors, and writes to the audit log."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Proxy Service","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Your public-facing gateway. It handles protocol detection and routing — the same port can serve the web UI, SSH connections, Kubernetes API requests, and database connections through Multiplex Mode."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kubernetes Service","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — The agent that connects Teleport to your Kubernetes cluster''s API server. It maintains a reverse tunnel back to the proxy, meaning your API server never needs to be exposed to the internet."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"whats-ahead"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What''s Ahead"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In this course, we''ll walk through deploying Teleport with Helm, setting up GitHub SSO, building fine-grained RBAC with defense in depth, implementing just-in-time access requests, and exploring audit logging and session recording."}]}]}', 1790105224000);
', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Getting Started', 2, '{"id":"bx2e90jd6p1u2pkvgj1wo6b7","thumbnailUrl":"https://content.rawkode.academy/videos/bx2e90jd6p1u2pkvgj1wo6b7/thumbnail.webp"}', NULL, NULL, 1774224000, 0, '["rawkode"]', '[{"title":"Teleport Helm Chart Documentation","description":"Official Teleport Helm chart installation guide","type":"url","url":"https://goteleport.com/docs/deploy-a-cluster/helm-deployments/","category":"documentation"}]', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"getting-hands-on"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Getting Hands On"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Time to get our hands on the keyboard and actually deploy our first Teleport cluster. By the end of this video, you will have a working Teleport cluster running on Kubernetes with TLS certificates and an admin user ready to log in."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"prerequisites"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Prerequisites"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You''ll need Kind (Kubernetes in Docker), Helm, and kubectl. No cloud account required — everything runs locally. The only dependency is cert-manager, which is just one "},{"type":"text","text":"helm install","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" away."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"deployment-steps"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deployment Steps"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The video walks through these deployment steps:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Check prerequisites","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Verify Kind, Helm, and kubectl are installed"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Create a Kind cluster","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — A simple single control-plane configuration"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deploy cert-manager","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Add the Jetstack Helm repository, install cert-manager with CRDs, create a self-signed issuer and root certificate"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Configure Teleport values","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Standalone chart mode, Multiplex proxy listener, and cert-manager integration"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Helm install","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Use "},{"type":"text","text":"envsubst","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" for values templating, then "},{"type":"text","text":"helm upgrade --install","marks":[{"type":"code"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"production-comparison"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Production Comparison"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We also take a look at a real production deployment running on rawkode.cloud using Flux CD, with ACME-managed TLS certificates, Mayastor storage, and Gateway API via Cilium. The production setup isn''t that dissimilar from the local Kind deployment — just a few tweaks for your specific infrastructure."}]}]}', 1790105224000);
', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Access Control', 3, '{"id":"s7mhnkw81wdgfe5tc4a2ky18","thumbnailUrl":"https://content.rawkode.academy/videos/s7mhnkw81wdgfe5tc4a2ky18/thumbnail.webp"}', NULL, NULL, 1774224000, 0, '["rawkode"]', '[{"title":"Teleport GitHub SSO Guide","description":"Configure GitHub as an identity provider for Teleport","type":"url","url":"https://goteleport.com/docs/access-controls/sso/github-sso/","category":"documentation"}]', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"moving-beyond-manual-users"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Moving Beyond Manual Users"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In the last video, we deployed Teleport and created a manual admin user. That''s fine for development, but not for production. Today we''re setting up GitHub as our identity provider so every user authenticates through SSO and gets a short-lived certificate tied to their real identity."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"github-sso-with-teleport"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"GitHub SSO with Teleport"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the Teleport Community Edition, the supported provider is GitHub — perfect for developers. Enterprise users can also use OIDC and SAML connectors for Okta, Azure AD, Google Workspace, and other identity providers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The flow is simple:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"User runs "},{"type":"text","text":"tsh login","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" to authenticate with Teleport"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It opens a browser for GitHub authentication"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Teleport issues a short-lived certificate"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"User can "},{"type":"text","text":"tsh kube ls","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" to list available clusters and start working"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"gitops-configuration"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"GitOps Configuration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We configure everything the GitOps way using Teleport''s operator and custom resource definitions:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Enable the Teleport operator","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" in the Helm release"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Create a GitHub connector","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" CRD with client ID, secret, and team-to-role mappings"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Define Teleport roles","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (access and editor) as custom resources"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"GitHub teams map directly to Teleport roles — anyone in the Rawkode Academy organization gets read access, platform team members get editor access."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"cli-access"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"CLI Access"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Once configured, "},{"type":"text","text":"tsh login --proxy=rawkode.cloud --auth github","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" authenticates via browser, and you''re in. "},{"type":"text","text":"tsh kube ls","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" shows available clusters, and kubectl works seamlessly through Teleport''s proxy — no kubeconfig files needed."}]}]}', 1786715451000);
', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Access Control', 4, '{"id":"h1qevuz6tezzpolx3afeo0r2","thumbnailUrl":"https://content.rawkode.academy/videos/h1qevuz6tezzpolx3afeo0r2/thumbnail.webp"}', NULL, NULL, 1774224000, 0, '["rawkode"]', '[{"title":"Teleport RBAC Documentation","description":"Configure role-based access control in Teleport","type":"url","url":"https://goteleport.com/docs/access-controls/guides/role-templates/","category":"documentation"}]', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"the-principle-of-least-privilege"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Principle of Least Privilege"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Having identity-based access is a massive improvement, but if every authenticated user has full cluster admin access, we still have a problem. Overprivileged access is one of the most common root causes in Kubernetes security incidents — the problem is the process, not the developer."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"two-layer-rbac-model"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two-Layer RBAC Model"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We build a defense-in-depth model with two independent layers:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Teleport roles","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" determine which Kubernetes groups are embedded in the user''s certificate, which clusters and namespaces the user can reach, and hard deny rules that override everything else"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kubernetes RBAC","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (roles and role bindings) controls what those groups are actually allowed to do within specific namespaces"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"namespace-boundaries"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Namespace Boundaries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We create development and production namespaces with corresponding Kubernetes roles:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dev viewers","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" get read-only access (get, list, watch) to pods, services, config maps, and deployments in the development namespace"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Prod admins","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" get full access to all resources in the production namespace"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"teleport-deny-rules"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Teleport Deny Rules"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The most important part: deny rules under "},{"type":"text","text":"spec.deny","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" explicitly block resources in specific namespaces. Deny rules in Teleport always take precedence over allow rules — there is no way to override them. This creates a hard security boundary even if someone misconfigures the Kubernetes side."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"defense-in-depth"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Defense in Depth"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both layers enforce the same boundary independently. If someone creates a Kubernetes cluster role binding that accidentally grants broader access, the Teleport deny rule still holds. If someone modifies the Teleport role but forgets Kubernetes, the namespace-scoped role still holds. Two independent layers, each capable of blocking unauthorized access on its own."}]}]}', 1786715451000);
', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Advanced Features', 5, '{"id":"c94kkvztdlqc5vvil0c91p95","thumbnailUrl":"https://content.rawkode.academy/videos/c94kkvztdlqc5vvil0c91p95/thumbnail.webp"}', NULL, NULL, 1774224000, 0, '["rawkode"]', '[{"title":"Teleport Access Requests","description":"Just-in-time access request documentation","type":"url","url":"https://goteleport.com/docs/access-controls/access-requests/","category":"documentation"}]', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"the-problem-with-standing-privileges"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Problem with Standing Privileges"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Role-based access control is a great foundation, but on-call engineers need the ability to respond to incidents at 3 AM. The traditional approach gives them permanent cluster admin access — a security liability. If credentials are compromised, the attacker inherits everything. From a compliance perspective, frameworks like SOC 2, FedRAMP, and ISO 27001 increasingly expect least privilege with time-bounded access."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"just-in-time-access"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Just-in-Time Access"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The idea is simple: nobody has admin access by default. When paged, you submit a request, explain why you need it, it gets peer-reviewed, and you get temporary access. Fix the issue, go back to bed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Teleport calls these access requests — a first-class feature built into the platform. No external ticketing system required, no custom code or scripts."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"implementation"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Implementation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We modify the existing dev team and prod team roles to add access request capabilities, plus create a new elevated "},{"type":"text","text":"kube-admin","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" role with a one-hour TTL:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Add ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"allow.request","marks":[{"type":"bold"},{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" to existing roles so users can request the "},{"type":"text","text":"kube-admin","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" role"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Create the ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"kube-admin","marks":[{"type":"bold"},{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" role","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" with system:masters access and a short TTL"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Request flow","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":": "},{"type":"text","text":"tsh request create --roles=kube-admin --reason=\"debugging production for client XYZ\"","marks":[{"type":"code"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Approval","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":": Admin approves via "},{"type":"text","text":"tctl request approve","marks":[{"type":"code"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Login with elevated access","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":": "},{"type":"text","text":"tsh login --request-id=<id>","marks":[{"type":"code"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Drop privileges","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":": "},{"type":"text","text":"tsh request drop","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" returns to normal access"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The one-hour TTL means even in the worst case, the window of elevated privilege is time-bounded. Tune this to your organization''s needs — 15 minutes, 30 minutes, or 6 hours for a maintenance period."}]}]}', 1786715451000);
', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Advanced Features', 6, '{"id":"twq63ce7cr33d3nn6olb2aa2","thumbnailUrl":"https://content.rawkode.academy/videos/twq63ce7cr33d3nn6olb2aa2/thumbnail.webp"}', NULL, NULL, 1774224000, 0, '["rawkode"]', '[{"title":"Teleport Audit Log","description":"Teleport audit logging and session recording documentation","type":"url","url":"https://goteleport.com/docs/reference/audit/","category":"documentation"}]', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"the-missing-piece-auditing"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Missing Piece: Auditing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We''ve deployed Teleport, wired up GitHub for SSO, built fine-grained RBAC with deny rules, and set up just-in-time access requests. The final piece of the puzzle is auditing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every time someone runs a kubectl command through Teleport, an audit event is created. Every login, every API call, every session start and end — it''s all recorded and tied to a real human identity from your SSO provider."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"the-audit-log"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Audit Log"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Teleport''s audit log captures everything happening within your cluster:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Certificate issuance events with SSO login details"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every Kubernetes API call with the user who made it"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Access request creation and approval events"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Failed login attempts and MFA failures"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"session-recording"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Session Recording"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"When someone runs "},{"type":"text","text":"kubectl exec","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" into a pod, Teleport records the entire session. You can play it back with "},{"type":"text","text":"tsh play <session-id>","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"type":"text","text":" to see exactly what commands were executed — invaluable for incident response and compliance."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"live-session-joining"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Live Session Joining"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Active sessions can be joined in real-time:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Observer mode","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (default): Watch someone else''s session without being able to type"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Peer mode","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":": Join as a collaborator and take over typing — perfect for pair debugging production issues"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is a feature we used every week when filming Klustered."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"course-summary"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Course Summary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Over these six videos, we''ve gone from a blank slate to a fully functional Teleport deployment with:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Identity-based access via GitHub SSO"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fine-grained RBAC with defense in depth"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Just-in-time access requests"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Audit logging and session recording"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Live session joining and peer programming"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything was built on the Teleport Community Edition, which is free and open source. There are several features worth exploring further — OIDC and SAML integration, SSH proxying, database connections, application proxying, and Windows desktop access — all through the same unified access plane with the same audit trail."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Check out the documentation at goteleport.com/docs to keep going."}]}]}', 1786715451000);
